✅ QUESTION 5: MINIMUM DAYS TO MAKE M BOUQUETS

(Binary Search on Answer)

🔹 Problem Statement

You are given an integer array bloomDay where bloomDay[i] is the day the i-th flower blooms.

You want to make m bouquets, and each bouquet needs k adjacent flowers.

Return the minimum number of days required to make all bouquets.
If it is not possible, return -1.

📌 Example 1
Input:
bloomDay = [1,10,3,10,2]
m = 3
k = 1

Output: 3

📌 Example 2
Input:
bloomDay = [1,10,3,10,2]
m = 3
k = 2

Output: -1

🔹 Why This Problem Is HARD

Greedy ❌

DP ❌

Sliding Window ❌

Binary Search on ANSWER ✅

👉 We are not searching in an array,
👉 we are searching in the range of days.

🔹 Key Insight 💡

If we can make m bouquets on day X,
then we can also make them on any day > X.

📌 This is a monotonic condition → Binary Search!

🔹 Algorithm

If m * k > bloomDay.length, return -1

Binary search between:

low = min(bloomDay)

high = max(bloomDay)

For each mid:

Check if it’s possible to make m bouquets

Return minimum valid day

🔹 Helper Function Logic

For a given day:

Count consecutive bloomed flowers

Every k consecutive flowers → 1 bouquet

🔹 Time & Space Complexity

Time: O(N log M)

Space: O(1)

🔹 Java Code (Interview-Perfect)
class Solution {

    public int minDays(int[] bloomDay, int m, int k) {

        if ((long) m * k > bloomDay.length) return -1;

        int low = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        int high = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

        for (int day : bloomDay) {
            low = Math.min(low, day);
            high = Math.max(high, day);
        }

        int answer = -1;

        while (low <= high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;

            if (canMake(bloomDay, m, k, mid)) {
                answer = mid;
                high = mid - 1;   // try smaller day
            } else {
                low = mid + 1;
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }

    private boolean canMake(int[] bloomDay, int m, int k, int day) {
        int flowers = 0;
        int bouquets = 0;

        for (int bloom : bloomDay) {
            if (bloom <= day) {
                flowers++;
                if (flowers == k) {
                    bouquets++;
                    flowers = 0;
                }
            } else {
                flowers = 0;
            }
        }
        return bouquets >= m;
    }
}

🔹 Line-by-Line Explanation
if (m * k > bloomDay.length)


Not enough flowers → impossible.

low = min(bloomDay), high = max(bloomDay)


Search space is days, not indices.

canMake(...)


Checks feasibility for a given day.

high = mid - 1


Try to find minimum day.

🔹 Worked Example
Input
bloomDay = [1,10,3,10,2]
m = 3, k = 1


Binary Search Days:

Day 3 → flowers bloomed = [1,3,2] → 3 bouquets ✔
Day 2 → only 2 bouquets ❌


👉 Answer = 3

